  {
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "If we do this, I think stochastic rounding has to be part of the MVP. \n\nI have questions / thoughts off the top of my head:\n* What's the coverage matrix here? What (dtype, rounding_mode) pairs do we care about? Do we support every rounding on every type? A more sparse support, like we do for `scaled_mm`? It feels like this should be more sparse, but that's not a technical necessity - one could emulate all roundings on all HW/types if necessary.\n* What's the effect on binary size going to be, or would this need to be done in a DSL? \n* What's the realistic !/$ here? Take RU for instance - how often is `scales.to(torch.float8_e8m0fnu, rounding=\"up\")` going to be called in PyT code vs. the rounding done in a fused CUDA kernel / DSL JIT kernel? We've got this far without needing to add the cross-product of dtypes x rounding modes, do we actually need them now, or do we just need a few cases like e8m0 + RU, fp{8,6,4} + RS? \n\nStochastic rounding has its own set of concerns / questions / issues w.r.t. RNG that'd have to worked through too. \n\nSo yeah, fine with the idea, but there's a bunch of things to reason through...",
