  {
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "The issue I see with stochastic rounding is that there's the \"reasonable\" way of doing it (based off first principles: iid uniform random sampling of as many bits as are being thrown away), and then there's NVIDIA's way of doing it (fewer bits than mathematically necessary, reuse of bits across values through reversing, ...).\n\nI see it as _extremely_ difficult to expose the NVIDIA PTX accelerated stochastic rounding through a generic clean API.\n\nAlso, I wanted to clarify that I don't really care about eager performance (if I did I could easily write my custom op in CuTeDSL), rather I want the rounding mode of `.to(...)` to be understood by Inductor and be lowered to the appropriate Triton, so that I can use this efficiently in fused kernels. I was thus going to suggest that we could start by exposing the rounding modes that are [supported by Triton](https://triton-lang.org/main/python-api/generated/triton.language.cast.html), but it seems that round-up isn't among them, which means we can't support ue8m0 casts. Perhaps we first need to add support for it in Triton?",
